    <fr:title text="Logical Frameworks as a meta-language">Logical Frameworks as a meta-language</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    While reading <fr:link href="/forest/von-neumann-1925/" title="An Axiomatization of Set Theory" uri="https://hejohns.github.io/forest/von-neumann-1925/" display-uri="von-neumann-1925" type="local">An Axiomatization of Set Theory</fr:link>, I just had the completely trivial-- and
    "that's… the point"-- "realization" that first order logic is the
    meta-language of axiomatic mathematics, just as (it seems) logical
    frameworks are the meta-languages of (typically λ-calculus based)
    programming languages. Well not literally, but the discrepancy is
    non-substantial.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    That is, (classical) FoL is often the setting in which axiomatic
    mathematical theories are formalized. Classic (no pun intended) examples
    are <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\mathcal {L}_{group} = \{e, ⋅, -⁻¹\}, Th(\textsf {groups}))]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\mathcal {L}_{DLO} = \{≤\}, Th(\textsf {Dense Linear Orders}))]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\mathcal {L}_{sets} = \{∈\}, ZFC)]]></fr:tex>.
    (To be ultra-pedantic is a fruitless way, I've paired the language and theory.)
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    "definite property"
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
